Dialogue: 0,0:43:25.51,0:43:29.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,But But I think there's a lot of cool stuff to be to be built there as well.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:29.60,0:43:31.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,You brought up snapshotting a couple times.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:31.48,0:43:34.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like what do you see as like the use case for snapshotting?
Dialogue: 0,0:43:34.54,0:43:37.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like Like what would that give us that we don't get right now?
Dialogue: 0,0:43:37.52,0:43:44.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think the the big thing for me is like today a lot of sandbox workloads are kind of like lift and shift.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:44.52,0:43:48.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like people think a lot about sandboxes almost like, you know, I have my dev box.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:48.60,0:43:50.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now I'm going to shove it in the cloud.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:50.07,0:43:51.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm going to have an agent control it.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:51.94,0:43:54.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Maybe I'll run the harness inside the sandbox.
Dialogue: 0,0:43:54.64,0:44:00.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,I don't know if like long-term that's like the right way to think about, you know, on like compute for agents.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:00.48,0:44:06.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I think a much better model would be if you're going to have this like very long-running agents,
Dialogue: 0,0:44:06.23,0:44:10.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,which almost like to me are like kind of durable execution workflows.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:10.87,0:44:13.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like they're going to run, you know, 24/7.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:13.28,0:44:18.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, every once in a while they're going to run commands and and you know, having the ability to like spin up a sandbox in,
Dialogue: 0,0:44:18.54,0:44:24.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, 100 ms, run a few commands, and then like shut it down and like snapshot it.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:24.38,0:44:29.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think the faster you can get that like, you know, wake up, you know, go to sleep cycle,
Dialogue: 0,0:44:29.08,0:44:33.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,the more you can sort of create sandboxes that only run for like a very brief period.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:33.52,0:44:35.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and that will make compute much more efficient.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:35.50,0:44:40.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it will also enable you to to to to do some really cool things, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:44:40.18,0:44:45.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,like forking and and moving things around and and and you know, having like imagine, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:44:45.85,0:44:50.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,like cloud code instead of you know, having sort of a very linear flow, like sequential flow,
Dialogue: 0,0:44:50.56,0:44:54.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,like kind of branching and you know, running hundreds of agents at the same time, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:44:54.38,0:44:57.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think a lot of like CI is like another like illustrative example.
Dialogue: 0,0:44:57.42,0:45:02.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like for most of the the history of CI, uh you know, I talk about like GitHub actions and things like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:02.35,0:45:03.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like you had a very sequential flow.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:03.87,0:45:10.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like you you you know, you slowly install all your stuff, then you run the test suite, and then you know, generate some reports or whatever.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:10.64,0:45:14.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it's like you can break it up into shards, but it's kind of annoying.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:14.24,0:45:19.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like what I would love to do is like take your entire test suite and just run every single test in its own sandbox, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:45:19.96,0:45:21.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like and just parallelize the out of that.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:21.80,0:45:23.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Run like a thousand sandboxes.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:23.78,0:45:25.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Why can't that be done?
Dialogue: 0,0:45:25.39,0:45:31.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean, the problem is like there's too much there's too much overhead around like, you know, starting and stopping sandboxes.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:31.07,0:45:36.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I think the more like you you create like, you know, frictionless like starting and stopping,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:36.05,0:45:44.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,the more you can enter a world where like you can do this like extremely bursty like large-scale like run as much as you think want in parallel.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:44.08,0:45:46.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,So so I you know, I'm an infra geek like I said.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:46.86,0:45:49.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like I I I get very excited about these things.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:49.32,0:45:52.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, once we figure out all these primitives and the right building blocks,
Dialogue: 0,0:45:52.63,0:45:57.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think it's going to unlock a lot of really cool stuff that, you know, we we previously could not do with with software.
Dialogue: 0,0:45:57.53,0:45:59.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,Why hasn't that already been built?
Dialogue: 0,0:45:59.23,0:46:04.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,I would assume even previously people would would want to parallelize as much of of their CI as possible.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:04.92,0:46:08.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can, but the overhead is like Amdahl's law whatever, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:46:08.68,0:46:13.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like the overhead of parallelization right now is very high cuz like right now like you can get up actions for instance.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:13.64,0:46:16.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like we we parallelize in in the modal test suite, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:46:17.00,0:46:22.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,But like what that basically means is like now if you take your test suite and you split it up in two,
Dialogue: 0,0:46:22.64,0:46:27.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,like now each of those shards both need to do all the installs and and the test setup.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:27.18,0:46:28.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that takes a long time.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:28.38,0:46:29.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like it it can take minutes.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:29.76,0:46:33.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,But let's say you can do the test setup once and then you snapshot the
Dialogue: 0,0:46:33.10,0:46:37.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,container and now you clone it and now you can run the test suites independently.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:37.30,0:46:42.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think a lot of a lot of the the benefits cannot be uh you can't
Dialogue: 0,0:46:42.26,0:46:46.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,materialize those benefits on on until you get the the friction and and the overhead down.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:46.84,0:46:47.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mhm.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:47.08,0:46:48.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,The transaction cost.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:48.32,0:46:53.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think you guys have a partnership with Anthropic and they're using sandboxes for something.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:53.24,0:46:54.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like modal sandboxes.
Dialogue: 0,0:46:54.76,0:47:01.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, I mean the the managed agents with with Anthropic this is sort of a bring your own sandbox integration.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:01.41,0:47:03.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,So you can you can use modal for those, yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:03.92,0:47:04.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Oh, cool.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:04.42,0:47:05.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,How did that come up?
Dialogue: 0,0:47:05.51,0:47:06.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like come to be?
Dialogue: 0,0:47:06.60,0:47:08.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think Anthropic is like trying to create, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:08.81,0:47:13.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,like a thriving ecosystem and community and and so I think, you know, they make money from inference, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:47:13.93,0:47:16.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like so I I I think it's very much in their interest to promote, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:16.97,0:47:21.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,like a for everything else, you know, as much of, you know, of an ecosystem as possible.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:21.08,0:47:25.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,They want to have the largest possible surface area as long as you use Anthropic for inference.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:25.07,0:47:30.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I I think it makes like very rational sense for them to to to partner with with us as a sandbox provider.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:30.16,0:47:34.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Also, I feel like we have to bring up the Hugging Face incident.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:34.06,0:47:35.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Are you okay talking about that?
Dialogue: 0,0:47:35.40,0:47:35.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:35.65,0:47:36.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, I mean it's all there.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:36.88,0:47:37.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:37.47,0:47:42.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,So yeah, could you just briefly describe like what happened and I think they were using the modal sandbox,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:42.16,0:47:45.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,but it wasn't your, you know, it wasn't like a fault of the sandbox.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:45.52,0:47:46.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:46.24,0:47:51.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I I don't I forget the all the details of of the exploit, but I mean, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:47:51.76,0:47:54.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think what you're referring to is the fact that like modal was involved.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:54.95,0:47:55.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:55.30,0:47:57.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, you know, just to comment on that.
Dialogue: 0,0:47:57.78,0:48:02.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like basically the the the agents running inside, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:02.23,0:48:09.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,that that that exploited hugging face they discovered a a vulnerability in an app that was deployed on Modal.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:09.24,0:48:09.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mhm.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:09.68,0:48:16.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,And they used that to sort of as almost like a springboard for for getting access, getting full internet access is my understanding.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:16.12,0:48:17.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,I don't remember the full details.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:17.35,0:48:21.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,To be clear this is an app running on Modal that was hacked.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:21.29,0:48:22.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like it was not Modal that was hacked.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:22.64,0:48:22.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:22.88,0:48:28.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,So it's like AWS a lot of people run stuff on AWS, occasionally those app get hacked.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:28.05,0:48:29.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,Does that mean AWS got hacked?
Dialogue: 0,0:48:29.46,0:48:31.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like no, of course not, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:48:31.06,0:48:35.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and so there's a lot of people running apps on Modal and you know, one of those apps got hacked.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:35.16,0:48:37.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I I say but but like there are some lessons we can learn.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:37.53,0:48:46.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and and I think one of the things we've been reflecting on is like could we have more sensible, you know, security restrictions by default.
Dialogue: 0,0:48:46.12,0:48:53.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think to use the AWS analogy in the as just just as another analogy you know, S3 used to be this like,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:53.26,0:48:59.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, it used to be very easy to to accidentally make an S3 bucket public and AWS realized like,
Dialogue: 0,0:48:59.09,0:49:02.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, it doesn't necessarily cause that you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:02.33,0:49:09.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,pose a security problem for AWS per se but a lot of their customers accidentally would, you know, like leak data.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:09.36,0:49:13.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so S3 it's much harder now to make S3 buckets public.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:13.25,0:49:15.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so in a similar way it's like we're exploring like, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:15.71,0:49:22.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,can't we make security a little bit more restricted by default without necessarily, you know, destroying the magic that make that makes Modal good.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:22.41,0:49:28.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I think this also, you know, it's a kind of a separate side track is like agents are getting really good at cyber security.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:28.78,0:49:32.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we've actually also as a sort of, you know, separate sort of learning from this incident.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:32.81,0:49:36.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like we started using agents internally quite a lot to to to try to find vulnerabilities.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:37.03,0:49:38.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,So far hasn't found anything,
Dialogue: 0,0:49:38.43,0:49:42.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,but like they're very good at like probing into like very deep parts of the
Dialogue: 0,0:49:42.90,0:49:48.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,system and exploiting trying to exploit things and trying to escalate and get out of sandboxes.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:48.88,0:49:52.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um so, you know, it's it's it's something we're exploring very actively right now.
Dialogue: 0,0:49:53.00,0:49:55.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,How exactly do you get the agents to like run through the code base?
Dialogue: 0,0:49:55.87,0:49:58.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're just like, "Hey, like find security vulner- vulnerabilities?"
Dialogue: 0,0:49:58.72,0:50:02.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Well, no, you just like you just put them in a sandbox and and tell them like, "Can you try to escape?"
Dialogue: 0,0:50:02.72,0:50:05.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,Just run through the Can you just try to escape from the sandbox?
Dialogue: 0,0:50:05.49,0:50:08.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's like escape the room, but for agents.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:08.56,0:50:08.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Cool.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:08.87,0:50:10.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,When did you start doing that?
Dialogue: 0,0:50:10.48,0:50:12.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,We started doing it a a few months ago.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:12.92,0:50:13.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,And it's pretty exciting.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:13.88,0:50:15.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like when stable, I guess, came out.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:15.08,0:50:16.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, roughly around the same time.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:16.56,0:50:17.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:17.08,0:50:23.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean, my my view on like cybersecurity is that, you know, I actually think they're very good at finding vulnerabilities,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:23.65,0:50:26.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,but I think you can also use them defensively well very well.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:26.90,0:50:31.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, I I I think, you know, companies like Model and many other companies right now, like I think, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:50:31.38,0:50:36.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,if if you go to talk to like pretty much every CISO, I'm sure they're all thinking about the same thing.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:36.29,0:50:41.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, how do we use agents defensively to to to, you know, fix all our vulnerabilities?
Dialogue: 0,0:50:41.06,0:50:43.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, over time, actually, in a way, like I'm not that nervous.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:43.86,0:50:51.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think if if anything, like agents and and cybersecurity, all the breakthroughs in cybersecurity, will will make the world safer, if anything.
Dialogue: 0,0:50:51.64,0:50:56.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,Do you think that like all of the cybersecurity the big cybersecurity companies are just cooked?
Dialogue: 0,0:50:56.66,0:51:04.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,I don't know if that's true because I mean, you're asking something that's like very far outside of my like core expertise.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:04.76,0:51:10.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,But when I think about like cybersecurity, a lot of what you're selling is effectively fear,
Dialogue: 0,0:51:10.30,0:51:16.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,and that's almost like a branding and like marketing strategy, as much as, you know, obviously, the core products.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:16.62,0:51:22.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I think, you know, brand and things like that are actually like arguably like more valuable in the era of AI.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:22.45,0:51:26.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, I I I wouldn't necessarily jump to the conclusion that they're cooked.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:26.45,0:51:31.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think they have, you know, deep relationships with the enterprise customers, and they have, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:51:31.76,0:51:36.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,big sales teams who are very good at telling, you know, spooky stories and and things like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:36.65,0:51:39.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,And And I think they're going to continue to be very good at that.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:39.05,0:51:41.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,I just asked cuz like Yeah, you're doing it yourself.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:41.13,0:51:44.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,You're not going to one of like the Palo Alto Networks or whatever.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:44.26,0:51:47.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I guess like it's only like giant enterprises that care about that.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:47.43,0:51:49.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,Also, they can blame somebody if something goes wrong. true.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:49.99,0:51:55.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, yeah, you kind of want to hire in many cases just like a established vendor uh in order to blame them.
Dialogue: 0,0:51:55.93,0:52:02.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I I mean, to some extent, it it does reduce a little bit the need for us to do to bring in pen testers like human pen testers,
Dialogue: 0,0:52:02.92,0:52:05.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,which most startups like do on a regular cycle.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:05.84,0:52:09.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, maybe previously, you know, we would do it I actually don't know what the cycle was,
Dialogue: 0,0:52:09.69,0:52:12.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,but but I think we used to do it like once every 6 months roughly.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:12.58,0:52:15.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Maybe now we only need to do it once every year.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:15.56,0:52:16.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I don't know.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:16.96,0:52:21.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,But but like I think with all of these things, you know, humans can also use them to get more efficiently.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:21.96,0:52:23.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, like, you know, like I I I I don't know.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:23.65,0:52:27.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm I'm kind of a believer also that like humans with AI is even more powerful now.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:28.04,0:52:34.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, like, maybe there's an argument that like we can bring in pen testers who are really good at using agents and,
Dialogue: 0,0:52:34.08,0:52:35.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, find vulnerability even faster.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:35.62,0:52:36.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I don't know.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:36.31,0:52:38.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I'm not that, you know, I'm an optimist.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:38.61,0:52:42.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,I started a company, so like, you know, I'm I'm generally like optimistic about everything,
Dialogue: 0,0:52:42.55,0:52:46.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,but like I I certainly don't think humans are cooked or companies are cooked.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:46.10,0:52:51.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, I I think in the era of AI, a lot of companies are going to figure out, you know, how to use AI to be even more efficient.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:51.20,0:52:51.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mhm.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:51.76,0:52:58.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,I want to transition a little bit to like developer experience cuz like early on I mean, even now, like you guys focus a lot on that.
Dialogue: 0,0:52:58.80,0:53:00.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you've talked a lot about that.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:00.32,0:53:02.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Does developer experience even matter anymore?
Dialogue: 0,0:53:02.88,0:53:04.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think it still matters.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:04.33,0:53:06.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and then I think you're you're sort of, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:06.64,0:53:11.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,like the context I think you're fish like the thing you're talking about is like now with agents, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:53:11.73,0:53:14.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like does developer experience Like the docs are already there.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:14.71,0:53:17.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,The agents are going to look at the docs, like all that stuff.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:17.24,0:53:24.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,To that point though, like almost all the things that make developer experience good also makes the agent experience good.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:24.60,0:53:28.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,Is kind of what we've seen in practice, which is like things like having good docs,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:28.84,0:53:33.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,having descriptive error messages, having fast feedback loops, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:53:33.88,0:53:36.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like I do think there's certain things have changed.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:36.56,0:53:42.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,The other day I was like setting something up with Cloudflare, and it's like I find Cloudflare like notoriously annoying to work with sometimes.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:43.01,0:53:49.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,and and I throw an agent at it and it was able to like just configure a bunch of like you know things and and balloon me for me.
Dialogue: 0,0:53:49.53,0:53:53.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you know and and so I will say like agents does they do make it a
Dialogue: 0,0:53:53.96,0:53:57.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,little bit easier to work with things that are like harder to work with in the past,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:57.70,0:53:58.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:53:58.27,0:54:02.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,like the sort of human toll of dealing with the animal has arguably gone down a
Dialogue: 0,0:54:02.84,0:54:06.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,little bit cuz you can just like you know tell your agent to deal with it.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:06.16,0:54:12.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,But but for the most part I think you know the things that make developer experience good also make agent experience good.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:12.24,0:54:19.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think to some extent also like infrastructure over time anyway becomes and needs to be more programmatic.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:19.40,0:54:23.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think that's always been the thesis about sandboxes is that you know like
Dialogue: 0,0:54:23.28,0:54:26.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,things that you previously had to configure using YAML that should just be an SDK.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:26.74,0:54:30.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,That should be more like imperative like launch a sandbox like do stuff with it.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:30.73,0:54:32.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,Tear the sandbox down, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:54:32.12,0:54:36.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like rather than sort of declarative using using you know some sort of DSL.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:36.17,0:54:41.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,It also pushes like a little bit of a different interface that maybe previously didn't exist.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:41.16,0:54:48.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think in either way like SDKs are as relevant as they were as they always were if not more.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:48.40,0:54:53.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Is there anything you changed in your product just because like you know most people are just running agents on on it?
Dialogue: 0,0:54:53.88,0:54:54.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,We do.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:54.38,0:54:58.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean we we also we actually do run a lot of internal tests where we have agents.
Dialogue: 0,0:54:58.99,0:55:04.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,We tell agents to like build a modal app and run it and I mean a very concrete thing that is
Dialogue: 0,0:55:04.83,0:55:11.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,kind of funny is that we actually seen that like because of agent training data it's very hard to deprecate things now.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:11.88,0:55:20.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,We see that like agents often like love using you know old versions of modal syntax and and it's kind of hard to nudge them away from that.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:20.83,0:55:24.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,So so we've actually had to be a little bit more conservative going forward like
Dialogue: 0,0:55:24.25,0:55:28.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know over the last year or so with with how we deprecate and remove features.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:28.54,0:55:32.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,It takes we have to be very careful to update every single example that's out there online.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:33.04,0:55:33.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:33.40,0:55:35.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and and you know Yeah, skills, right?
Dialogue: 0,0:55:35.12,0:55:36.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, there's like stuff like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:36.13,0:55:41.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,But but like there's also an insane amount of like modal code that's now like online too that goes into the training data.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:41.96,0:55:47.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,I've seen like that to be like kind of an you know a little bit of like an interesting example where like another thing by the way
Dialogue: 0,0:55:48.04,0:55:51.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,too is like there's obviously like to sort of similar to like search
Dialogue: 0,0:55:51.74,0:55:57.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,engine optimization there's like agent engine you know whatever people call it agent optimization.
Dialogue: 0,0:55:57.12,0:56:04.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,We often like run internal benchmarks where like ask a an agent like hey like can you deploy stable diffusion for us?
Dialogue: 0,0:56:05.01,0:56:09.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Use whatever tool you want and and we see if it recommends modal or not and those things are very useful for
Dialogue: 0,0:56:09.55,0:56:15.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,us as it attract over time or like hey spin up some sandboxes or like hey like rank all the sandbox vendors.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:15.67,0:56:18.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,So so we we spend a lot of time like doing doing those things to to to make sure.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:18.88,0:56:20.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean what do you do about that?
Dialogue: 0,0:56:20.23,0:56:23.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah you say hey like you know build this stable diffusion thing does it recommend modal or not?
Dialogue: 0,0:56:23.96,0:56:25.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like what yeah what do you do about that?
Dialogue: 0,0:56:25.51,0:56:30.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah I I think that's harder as I think you know it's like search engine optimization I
Dialogue: 0,0:56:30.29,0:56:35.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,think it's primarily through content is like you just have to put out a lot of content.
Dialogue: 0,0:56:35.17,0:56:43.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,You can also to some extent like sort of see the perception I think a little bit more like you can ask an agent like you know how
Dialogue: 0,0:56:43.60,0:56:52.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,does you know rank all the sandbox vendors by reliability or rank all the sandbox vendors by price and and sort of see and it does feel like agents
Dialogue: 0,0:56:52.82,0:57:01.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,are a little bit more prone to like mis understanding pricing and and you know things like that so we also spend a lot of time like just
Dialogue: 0,0:57:01.32,0:57:05.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,making content more clear and more like you know understandable for agents cuz we
Dialogue: 0,0:57:05.92,0:57:09.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,know they're all indexing like everything that's out there and and that's talking about modal.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:09.52,0:57:16.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Have you thought about just like making a bunch of GitHub repos and having the agents like write a bunch of modal code in there?
Dialogue: 0,0:57:16.35,0:57:17.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Have you tried that?
Dialogue: 0,0:57:17.24,0:57:23.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Slop cannon I think the truth is like that is probably something you can do.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:23.42,0:57:30.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,My guess is like LM training data today is probably quite insensitive to like what's like AI output
Dialogue: 0,0:57:31.04,0:57:35.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,in the first place and you can probably like create a lot of slop like we thought about
Dialogue: 0,0:57:35.64,0:57:39.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,it like I don't love that idea but like in theory like much like you know SEO in
Dialogue: 0,0:57:39.28,0:57:43.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,the early days and still to some extent like auto generated content is like a very effective strategy.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:44.00,0:57:47.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think it's possible like you know you could you can do those things too.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:47.45,0:57:48.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:48.08,0:57:50.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,I want to switch to FDEs.
Dialogue: 0,0:57:50.08,0:57:54.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Apparently you guys hire like forward deployed engineers like what do they do?
Dialogue: 0,0:57:54.56,0:58:00.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're in a new era of like building applications and and and you know things that we couldn't build you know just
Dialogue: 0,0:58:00.62,0:58:08.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,a few years ago and we see a lot of companies that you know they need a little bit of help right?
Dialogue: 0,0:58:08.17,0:58:15.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like you know and so we started investing in FDEs a couple years ago but it but it's really been this year we've kind of scaled it up a lot.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:15.26,0:58:19.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think we started this year having like four FDEs now we I think we have like 24 or something like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:19.71,0:58:25.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we've grown a lot and they do a you know it's more it's not exactly the pounder model but they do a
Dialogue: 0,0:58:25.26,0:58:31.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,lot of like heavy you know sort of customer engagement like they work with a lot of customers on performance tuning especially with LLMs.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:31.49,0:58:35.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,We we see it out there's so much benefit from like you know sitting down with
Dialogue: 0,0:58:35.16,0:58:41.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,customers and tuning draft models and tweaking uh you know the parameters in DLM and SULang.
Dialogue: 0,0:58:41.79,0:58:47.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um they often you know fix bugs or or not fix bugs but like you know when customers
Dialogue: 0,0:58:47.20,0:58:50.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,run into issues like they often work with customers trying to figure out like how do we work around
Dialogue: 0,0:58:50.96,0:58:56.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,that or uh another thing we we've used FDEs a lot for is like just kind of proactively you
Dialogue: 0,0:58:56.32,0:59:02.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,know if if we see certain customers you know over provisioning with model like customers requesting too much CPU
Dialogue: 0,0:59:02.56,0:59:06.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,or too much memory for instance like that's kind of expensive and like we we try to catch
Dialogue: 0,0:59:06.12,0:59:09.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,those things and we try to sit down with those customers like yo like you're spending a lot of
Dialogue: 0,0:59:09.57,0:59:13.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,money on model right now we can actually help you save money and then of course like with with
Dialogue: 0,0:59:13.83,0:59:19.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,sort of active deals you know we we help them migrate code from other platforms uh things like that.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:19.90,0:59:26.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um I think you know we we we never have the intention of becoming like a service heavy business.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:26.75,0:59:34.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I think the the goal for FDEs is like, you know, A is to be part of you know, the the sort of sales process and like closing deals.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:34.40,0:59:38.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,B is to serve customers and and do some like, you know, customer support.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:38.58,0:59:41.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,But like I actually think that like the most important thing is like C is
Dialogue: 0,0:59:41.60,0:59:47.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,like bridging gaps in the product that we later want to turn into like real features.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:47.84,0:59:52.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I I think to a large extent, you know, anything an FDE does, you know,
Dialogue: 0,0:59:52.60,0:59:57.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,my question is always like long-term, how do we turn this into products so we don't need an FDE for this?
Dialogue: 0,0:59:58.04,0:59:58.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,0:59:58.49,1:00:00.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Do you guys hire PMs?
Dialogue: 0,1:00:00.16,1:00:00.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,Product managers?
Dialogue: 0,1:00:01.04,1:00:01.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:01.36,1:00:06.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,No. Would you say these FDEs are kind of like pseudo-ish PMs?
Dialogue: 0,1:00:06.88,1:00:08.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's a little bit of a different function.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:08.17,1:00:13.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like I I think certainly on the more like, you know, customer-facing side they are, but there's like, you know, on the more like inward,
Dialogue: 0,1:00:13.60,1:00:19.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, internal side like there's a sort of separate part of like product management which is more like, what are the next building blocks?
Dialogue: 0,1:00:19.19,1:00:20.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,What else should I invest in?
Dialogue: 0,1:00:20.28,1:00:24.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,And like, you know, and then there's like something in in in between like, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:00:24.14,1:00:29.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,looking at like maybe 10 20 different customers that are all asking for like slightly different things, but maybe what are the patterns?
Dialogue: 0,1:00:29.96,1:00:31.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like how do we turn this into product?
Dialogue: 0,1:00:31.64,1:00:35.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,We do have people doing product with us and and it's sort of evolved, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:00:35.96,1:00:42.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,we've had people basically like become product managers after previously being either dev rel or engineers
Dialogue: 0,1:00:42.44,1:00:48.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,and they're showing strong aptitude on on doing thinking very strategically about what we have to build.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:48.05,1:00:51.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I'm actually not like anti-product management.
Dialogue: 0,1:00:51.07,1:00:59.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,I do think that a lot of companies in the past hired product management product managers too eagerly and and
Dialogue: 0,1:00:59.25,1:01:03.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think there's a risk that you you kind of I can sort of see it in a lot of products.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:03.68,1:01:08.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like certain products like you can tell like there's just like eager product managers
Dialogue: 0,1:01:08.48,1:01:12.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,that have just thought of like kind of like widgety things and just shipped them.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:12.22,1:01:19.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think doing product management at an infra company really well is very very hard cuz I think it takes like a very deep,
Dialogue: 0,1:01:19.09,1:01:23.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, level of strategic thinking, thinking about like what are the foundational building blocks?
Dialogue: 0,1:01:23.99,1:01:25.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Where do we want to be in 1 to 2 years?
Dialogue: 0,1:01:26.00,1:01:27.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,Cuz the truth is building infrastructure is very hard.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:28.02,1:01:34.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,And how do we make sure we don't end up with like 35 features that are like kind of similar-looking,
Dialogue: 0,1:01:34.38,1:01:37.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,but all do like a little bit slightly different things?
Dialogue: 0,1:01:37.44,1:01:41.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's sort of level of thinking is something that, you know, is very rare to find.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:41.73,1:01:45.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then for that reason, we haven't aggressively tried to build up a product management team yet.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:45.48,1:01:46.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, that makes sense.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:46.52,1:01:51.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,How would you say your teams have been evolving maybe like since Gen AI?
Dialogue: 0,1:01:51.06,1:01:54.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,Cuz I would assume Yeah, I mean, you obviously worked at like Spotify.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:54.22,1:01:56.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,You had like a certain team structure.
Dialogue: 0,1:01:56.17,1:02:00.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um have your team structures like evolved a lot now that, you know, we have coding agents?
Dialogue: 0,1:02:00.80,1:02:02.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,In a funny way, like yes.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:02.13,1:02:07.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I do think team structures have changed a lot, but I'm actually not convinced it was like Gen AI driving that.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:07.31,1:02:12.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think in particular, like one thing I've seen a lot is that there's been sort of a focus,
Dialogue: 0,1:02:12.35,1:02:18.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,like a swing back towards being an IC, that I actually think is very, very positive for the space as a whole.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:18.88,1:02:23.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think, you know, if you look back at the space like 10 years ago, 20 years ago,
Dialogue: 0,1:02:23.48,1:02:29.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,everyone wanted to be in management cuz that was the way you you would get more money and more, you know, more recognition and more prestige.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:30.02,1:02:36.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think that shift actually started even before Gen AI, but I do think it's like heavily accelerated with AI.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:36.50,1:02:44.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, you know, we we we make it very clear to everyone at Model that like manager or IC, it's like there's no difference in prestige.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:44.42,1:02:47.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like everyone is just a member of technical staff.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:47.28,1:02:51.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, it's about ultimately your contributions and we see a lot of ex-managers,
Dialogue: 0,1:02:51.81,1:02:55.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, becoming ICs and and cuz they want to go back and build code.
Dialogue: 0,1:02:55.65,1:03:01.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so and you know, and there's been there's been sort of shift away from like, you know, non-technical managers.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:01.26,1:03:02.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Everyone pretty including me.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:02.80,1:03:08.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I still write a lot of code at Model and and actually too and and pretty much everyone in in the tech team.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:08.20,1:03:11.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a lot of people who are not in the tech team still write code.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:11.52,1:03:13.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that to me is like, you know, a recent phenomenon.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:13.88,1:03:17.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,But but I actually don't know if it's like necessarily caused by Gen AI,
Dialogue: 0,1:03:17.64,1:03:24.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,but I think GenAI has like greatly accelerated it cuz it it made it even lower the cost of like writing code is even easier.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:25.04,1:03:30.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,So if you have like non-technical people like committing code, like how do you how do you review that?
Dialogue: 0,1:03:30.26,1:03:32.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like how do you guys do code reviews?
Dialogue: 0,1:03:32.48,1:03:36.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,We do code reviews, but it's we we think of it as optional.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:36.09,1:03:41.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean for most my view is like code reviews in in large part is like people
Dialogue: 0,1:03:41.56,1:03:47.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,in many cases trying to just escape the the accountability of like you know merging things into production.
Dialogue: 0,1:03:47.74,1:03:55.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and if you have that like, you know, if you have that culture like then to me that's a sign that you need to invest way more in,
Dialogue: 0,1:03:55.07,1:04:02.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, CD and and CI and and and things like that is CI in particular, but also CD continuous integration continuous deployment.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:02.26,1:04:05.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,We do code reviews like I mean like when I write code like occasionally like I'm
Dialogue: 0,1:04:05.78,1:04:09.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm asking you know authors like is this in line with how you think about things?
Dialogue: 0,1:04:09.93,1:04:12.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,I hope I'm not changing the architecture fundamentally.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:13.04,1:04:16.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,But we also make it clear internally that like code reviews are are quite optional.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:17.00,1:04:20.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you know, if you're just changing docs, if you're just doing, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:04:20.14,1:04:24.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,some basic stuff like don't wait for a human to look at it especially in the era of AI.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:24.40,1:04:26.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,Why do you need a human to look at every piece of code?
Dialogue: 0,1:04:26.86,1:04:27.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,You don't have to.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:27.82,1:04:34.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,But that does require you to have, you know, very good controls, you know, internally around, you know, testing things.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:34.40,1:04:40.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, and exactly like, you know, in for companies like we we need one more nine than the average startup, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:04:40.64,1:04:41.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,In terms of uptime.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:41.66,1:04:44.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,But like, you know, these are investments you just have to make.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:44.62,1:04:50.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like you have to build systems that are very resilient to like, you know, you have to have rate limits everywhere.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:50.44,1:04:51.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,You have to decouple systems.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:51.82,1:04:55.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,You have to make sure that like nothing is a single point of failure.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:55.36,1:04:57.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,You have to have extremely good test coverage.
Dialogue: 0,1:04:57.84,1:05:00.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Agents are actually really good at writing tests these days which was like,
Dialogue: 0,1:05:00.48,1:05:03.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, a few years ago I thought like one of the major things missing in agents.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:03.58,1:05:08.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now they're actually quite good at like cuz I think it takes a little bit of like sort of kind of like need to
Dialogue: 0,1:05:08.98,1:05:14.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,step out of what you're trying to do for a second and be almost a little bit like devil's advocate to write good tests.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:14.28,1:05:16.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,But now they're actually quite good at writing tests, too.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:16.08,1:05:18.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, I think I think all these things help.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:18.20,1:05:23.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,Do you guys use like frontier models for coding or do you guys have you guys experimented with uh like open-source ones?
Dialogue: 0,1:05:24.04,1:05:25.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,We experiment with everything.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:25.13,1:05:31.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,But yeah, I mean, you know, initially it was a lot of cursor then, you know, of course Claude and Codex and and now a lot of people are using,
Dialogue: 0,1:05:31.05,1:05:34.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, night shift and and all these open-source stuff or or other stocks.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:34.60,1:05:36.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, I don't necessarily have like a strong point of view.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:37.00,1:05:39.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like I want people to experiment and try different things.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:39.20,1:05:42.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like we, you know, use frontier models, use open-source models.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:42.14,1:05:45.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,We try to do a little bit more dog fooding internally now that we're actually
Dialogue: 0,1:05:45.18,1:05:48.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,running inference for a lot of the open-source models like at a very large scale.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:48.96,1:05:53.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think it's also helpful to like use our internal endpoints for a lot of agentic development.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:53.92,1:05:55.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh so, we try to promote that a little bit.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:55.84,1:05:56.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Mhm.
Dialogue: 0,1:05:56.72,1:06:00.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Something I read maybe like, I don't know, a year ago, maybe like 2 years ago,
Dialogue: 0,1:06:00.62,1:06:05.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,you were saying that you didn't think that there was going to be like a big on-prem business for you guys.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:06.00,1:06:09.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um have you maybe like changed your opinion on that?
Dialogue: 0,1:06:09.72,1:06:12.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,No, I have not changed my opinion.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:12.17,1:06:15.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um I think more or less to me, I'm a cloud maximalist.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:15.89,1:06:24.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,And maybe to take a step back, like when when the cloud came in 2008, 2009, or whatever, my first reaction was like, "That's crazy.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:24.66,1:06:28.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like why would I want to take my code and run it on someone else's computer?
Dialogue: 0,1:06:28.31,1:06:29.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's like insane."
Dialogue: 0,1:06:29.72,1:06:31.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then like 6 months later I was doing it.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:31.31,1:06:34.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I think, you know, like took me 6 months to change my mind.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:34.98,1:06:38.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,It took probably enterprises like another 15 to 20 years.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:38.23,1:06:43.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,But like you look at, you know, vendors like Snowflake or I mean AWS and like you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:06:43.05,1:06:48.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I think there's many examples of DataDog, etc. Like the cloud is clearly winning, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:06:48.85,1:06:52.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like and and the multi-tenant systems are clearly winning.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:52.60,1:06:59.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,And, you know, to me on-prem looks increasingly like sort of a legacy business model.
Dialogue: 0,1:06:59.84,1:07:07.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and I actually think, you know, BYOC, you know, sort of VPC peering, like all of these things, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:07:07.09,1:07:14.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,like to me they they reflect sort of an outdated security model of like we need to protect everything inside our own network, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:07:14.91,1:07:17.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like using the network as a moat.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:17.48,1:07:20.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that I don't think is true anything in anymore, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:07:20.28,1:07:28.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like you we've sort of moved on to more like actually robust security paradigms like, you know, zero trust and things like that.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:28.18,1:07:34.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so to me BYOC and those things like they they look increasingly as like outdated, at least from a security perspective.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:35.04,1:07:40.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,From our perspective also like the fact that we have full control over our entire infrastructure stack, we rent all the hardware,
Dialogue: 0,1:07:40.98,1:07:49.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,we deploy, we manage all the infrastructure, we deploy and like manage health and observe, you know, all the metrics on all the hosts.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:49.28,1:07:52.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,It means we can iterate very fast and build a greater product.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:52.25,1:07:54.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I think that's very very valuable versus, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:07:54.89,1:07:59.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,customers in the past who had enterprise editions that they had to deploy on customers environment.
Dialogue: 0,1:07:59.61,1:08:00.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,They had to be very, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:08:00.81,1:08:07.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,they had to think very carefully about release management and and having depend like like we don't care like if we want to introduce a new database,
Dialogue: 0,1:08:07.16,1:08:09.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,like we can introduce a new database cuz we have control over it.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:09.92,1:08:12.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like we don't need to think about what customers have access to.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:12.80,1:08:17.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,So to me multi-tenancy is is it has so many different benefits.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:17.56,1:08:20.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean, that being said like, you know, if we were to work with like, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:08:21.04,1:08:28.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,some very large Fortune 500 customer in the future and, you know, they ask for BYOC, you know, we've done this in the past.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:28.85,1:08:30.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,We'll give them a number.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:30.68,1:08:35.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, you know, that number is very high and no one said yes to it so far, but like who knows?
Dialogue: 0,1:08:35.41,1:08:38.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, you know, I at the right price I I I would still build it.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:38.40,1:08:43.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and I think there's like maybe separately like another thing which I think is interesting which is
Dialogue: 0,1:08:43.16,1:08:48.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,like in theory BYOC might also be a way to work with and and get capacity in certain ways.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:48.52,1:08:54.81,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like we we sometimes work with Neo Labs and those Neo Labs have already have very large capacity reservations.
Dialogue: 0,1:08:54.86,1:08:59.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,And, you know, the question is like, you know, how how do we how do we get them to run Modal when they already have capacity?
Dialogue: 0,1:08:59.65,1:09:01.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,And BYOC might be a solution.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:01.36,1:09:04.33,Sub,,0,0,0,,We can still keep the control plane in in our environment.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:04.38,1:09:09.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,And the other flip side of that is like we could maybe even run like other people's stuff on that capacity.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:09.21,1:09:12.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,So so there's some like kind of fun stuff you can do in theory where like
Dialogue: 0,1:09:12.36,1:09:16.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,you can sort of you know like kind of aggregate all that capacity into one big pool.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:16.34,1:09:18.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,So we're excited about some of those ideas.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:18.34,1:09:21.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,But in general like I feel like on prem I don't know if we're ever going to do.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:21.37,1:09:22.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,But BYOC like maybe.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:22.32,1:09:23.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,Bring your own cloud.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:23.35,1:09:24.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,I keep saying BYOC.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:24.23,1:09:26.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,I should explain I should explain the acronyms.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:26.68,1:09:32.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um let's say hypothetically you are going to raise a a ton more money like soon.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:32.92,1:09:35.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,What would you use that money for?
Dialogue: 0,1:09:35.28,1:09:39.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean like I mentioned like in in this capacity situation the a lot of the
Dialogue: 0,1:09:39.64,1:09:45.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,money a lot of the cash flow I should say that's required is is capacity prepayments,
Dialogue: 0,1:09:45.17,1:09:45.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,right?
Dialogue: 0,1:09:45.70,1:09:50.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and so that to me represents the best use of of of cash today.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:50.10,1:09:53.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh we're ramping up capacity the capacity very aggressively.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:54.00,1:09:58.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Maybe we move into you know other types of deployment models in the future, ownership or whatever.
Dialogue: 0,1:09:58.78,1:10:03.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I think there's always going to be a cash component of that, like some sort of down payment or prepayment or some sort of that.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:03.98,1:10:07.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,So that to me is is the the greatest use of cash right now.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:07.52,1:10:11.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,There's of course other things like you know salaries and office spaces and stuff like that.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:11.72,1:10:17.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,But that actually in the grand scheme of like our our financials like it's is basically a rounding error, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:17.44,1:10:18.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, interesting.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:18.20,1:10:20.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Do you think that's going to change?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:20.16,1:10:25.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,No. I I think you know the the sort of operating leverage that we have in this business is kind of insane.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:25.43,1:10:27.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like you know, we have 200 employees.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:27.24,1:10:33.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, they cost a lot of money, but you know, the the the GPUs cost a lot more money.
Dialogue: 0,1:10:33.28,1:10:40.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,If you had to say like what are the top like I don't know like three things that are your biggest focus or the business's biggest focus right now?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:40.33,1:10:41.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like what would you say those are?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:41.88,1:10:47.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,Capacity and the sort of financial complexity and you know, in general like how do we how do we think about that space?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:47.91,1:10:49.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,How do we think about pricing and packaging?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:49.72,1:10:53.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,How do we think about the sort of tension between ownership and renting?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:53.49,1:10:56.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Should we be going in a more neo cloud direction?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:56.28,1:10:57.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,Should we go in the opposite direction?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:57.92,1:10:59.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,This sort of counter precision?
Dialogue: 0,1:10:59.20,1:11:01.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like that's probably the biggest thing that I think a lot about.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:01.53,1:11:06.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh the second biggest thing I think a lot about is um probably around like more like product strategy.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:06.60,1:11:09.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like I think a lot about like what is the next building blocks?
Dialogue: 0,1:11:09.09,1:11:13.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like if if we you know do more like agentic stuff, like what are the things we need?
Dialogue: 0,1:11:13.34,1:11:13.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know,
Dialogue: 0,1:11:13.88,1:11:19.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,are there certain storage primitives or compute primitives or in general like what is the thing
Dialogue: 0,1:11:19.29,1:11:22.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,that the world is going to wake up and realize they need over the next few years?
Dialogue: 0,1:11:23.00,1:11:25.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and ideally we have that at that point.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:25.20,1:11:28.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,We've been pretty good in general at like you know anticipating demand and
Dialogue: 0,1:11:28.56,1:11:31.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,building products in advance of it with the serverless functions in the sandboxes.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:31.95,1:11:33.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,So what's the next thing?
Dialogue: 0,1:11:33.16,1:11:38.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,Then I would say like actually the third thing I think a lot about is like more around like culture and company building and stuff like that.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:38.64,1:11:40.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean we recently crossed 200 people.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:40.24,1:11:42.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're we're growing extremely fast.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:42.29,1:11:44.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,We'll probably hit 250 by the end of the year.
Dialogue: 0,1:11:44.59,1:11:46.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like when you're growing very fast,
Dialogue: 0,1:11:46.52,1:11:51.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,you have 250 people that are all trying to figure out how they can add
Dialogue: 0,1:11:51.39,1:11:54.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,the most value to Modal and you know making sure that they have the right context,
Dialogue: 0,1:11:54.76,1:11:56.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,that they have the right motivation,
Dialogue: 0,1:11:56.57,1:12:04.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,that they're excited to come into work every day cuz they get to build something incredibly cool and they know you know what makes Modal successful,
Dialogue: 0,1:12:04.43,1:12:05.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,what they can do,
Dialogue: 0,1:12:05.56,1:12:11.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,aligning the incentives and and making sure that you know we we we people have like a
Dialogue: 0,1:12:11.50,1:12:15.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,very positive culture where they have this sort of implicit trust to do a lot of stuff,
Dialogue: 0,1:12:15.98,1:12:22.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,but also the accountability to like you know deliver the best things and you know and and be held accountable to like if they're not doing it.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:22.44,1:12:25.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,To me those are like you know super critical things around this scale.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:25.99,1:12:29.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like when when you're like 10, 20, 30 people,
Dialogue: 0,1:12:29.20,1:12:34.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,you can all just like kind of sit in a room and like it's all kind of implicit and you kind of just like feel it.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:34.49,1:12:36.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,People can sort of read read me.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:36.32,1:12:38.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like they can tell me when I get a little bit annoyed.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:38.49,1:12:39.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,They can tell when I'm happy.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:39.99,1:12:45.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,But like now we're 250 people and like people don't necessarily see it and you know of course there's like a lot of new people.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:45.77,1:12:49.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,They're like maybe a little bit nervous to like talk to the CEO or like all this stuff.
Dialogue: 0,1:12:49.22,1:12:53.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,So like you know how do I remain approachable and how do I like it's it's there's
Dialogue: 0,1:12:53.43,1:12:57.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,like the Dunbar number which I think is like more sort of you know fake than than anything
Dialogue: 0,1:12:57.62,1:13:01.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,like at least like the story behind it but like it is actually kind of real still
Dialogue: 0,1:13:01.52,1:13:07.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,like there's something around like 150-200 where like laying the foundation of the culture is very very important.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:07.08,1:13:09.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,How many people are you guys trying to hire over the next year?
Dialogue: 0,1:13:09.92,1:13:13.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,We've grown about 4x on an annualized basis in the last 12 months.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:13.63,1:13:17.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think that's like an unsustainable growth rate on a relative basis.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:17.73,1:13:25.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,I've seen that you know you can double the team every year and that's not super hard to do but
Dialogue: 0,1:13:25.20,1:13:30.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,4x what you start to see is you know cultural drift you start to see islands of different working styles.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:30.97,1:13:36.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,You start to see people doing like very speculative things like that that's something I always watch out for in the engineering team.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:36.24,1:13:40.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,You go and talk to the engine an engineer and I'm like what are you working on and and it turns
Dialogue: 0,1:13:40.24,1:13:45.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,out they're like building something like kind of like fringe and like is this really what you should spend time on?
Dialogue: 0,1:13:45.40,1:13:49.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,So growing very fast has that cost and you know has the cost of ramping people up.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:49.60,1:13:50.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I I don't know.
Dialogue: 0,1:13:50.15,1:13:54.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I think by the end of the year maybe 250 by the end of the next year you know but but like you know we're going
Dialogue: 0,1:13:54.69,1:14:00.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,to keep growing like maybe two and a half 3x so so it's very likely we'll end up like next year like 700 people or something like that.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:00.72,1:14:02.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,And I'm going to assume most of those are engineers.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:02.56,1:14:03.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Most of the engineers.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:03.48,1:14:05.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're ramping up sales a lot too.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:05.56,1:14:06.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:06.09,1:14:08.27,Sub,,0,0,0,,What kinds of engineers?
Dialogue: 0,1:14:08.32,1:14:09.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's primarily systems engineers.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:09.85,1:14:13.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean we've always been like very deep into sort of you know Linux and
Dialogue: 0,1:14:13.44,1:14:19.49,Sub,,0,0,0,,file systems and scalable systems and networking and and cloud and and stuff like that.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:19.54,1:14:23.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,So so that's that's that's the core of what we're looking for like people are really good at those things.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:23.92,1:14:30.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're also looking a lot at like you know more like cuz we moved up in the stack a little bit like we're doing a lot of work in the LM infra space.
Dialogue: 0,1:14:30.58,1:14:41.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,So now we're dealing with a separate set of concerns around you know CUDA and like GPUs and and and and MFU you know like maxing out the tokens that
Dialogue: 0,1:14:41.24,1:14:49.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,you can get per GPU and and and a lot of that things and you know even though I actually like will say a lot of the work in that space,
Dialogue: 0,1:14:49.31,1:14:50.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,in my opinion,
Dialogue: 0,1:14:50.16,1:14:57.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,turns out it's like more systems-driven still cuz it turns out like KV caching and routing is actually like is still like very like systems-like,
Dialogue: 0,1:14:57.96,1:15:00.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,like like these are very like systems-heavy problems.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:00.93,1:15:04.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,But yeah, a little bit more like sort of GPU performance, that kind of stuff.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:04.13,1:15:06.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're starting to build up a research team.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:06.16,1:15:10.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're very excited about, you know, moving into stuff around like, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:15:10.46,1:15:17.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,post training and and distillation and and better methods for for quantization and things like that. or for internally?
Dialogue: 0,1:15:17.76,1:15:18.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,Both.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:17.98,1:15:26.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,I I think it's like a little bit like with the FTEs, like I I I think we initially will do a lot of stuff with our customers to optimize their models,
Dialogue: 0,1:15:26.12,1:15:29.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,but over time figure out how do we turn those into products.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:29.99,1:15:37.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think we'll sort of arguably fail in a certain way if every time a customer needs to optimize a model,
Dialogue: 0,1:15:37.08,1:15:43.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,we have to go and work with them and, you know, long-term it has to be turned into products.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:43.18,1:15:49.24,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and so with the FTEs and with the research team, initially you're going to have to do a lot of work with the customers, and we are at that.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:49.29,1:15:55.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,But over time we're also building a lot of internal tooling and a lot of overtime internal products that are fully self-serve.
Dialogue: 0,1:15:55.48,1:16:00.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh so we're working a lot of stuff right now in in the post training space, but also in in the GPU performance space.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:01.04,1:16:03.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,So a lot of cool stuff, a lot of cool stuff.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:03.44,1:16:07.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Back to the product strategy stuff that you were talking about.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:07.25,1:16:08.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Um yeah, like what are your thoughts?
Dialogue: 0,1:16:08.96,1:16:11.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like what do we need over the next year, 2 years?
Dialogue: 0,1:16:11.80,1:16:13.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think it's incredibly hard to say.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:13.42,1:16:19.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and if I said like I know exactly what we need to build, like I I think I would would be like delusional.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:19.72,1:16:22.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think what what I've what we've seen so far, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:16:22.30,1:16:26.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,and and what I kind of believe in on a more general level is like we need better building blocks.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:27.04,1:16:35.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like this is a new era of of software, and we're unlocking a whole new type of applications that could not be built a few years ago.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:35.52,1:16:44.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, doing, you know, all the GenAI stuff, code that, you know, LLMs that output code that, you know, it needs to run like in an isolated way.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:44.87,1:16:49.20,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, there's so many like new primitives that are needed to to work with GPUs and work with LLMs.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:49.24,1:16:53.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,And, you know, we built some building blocks so far and and, you know, that's unlocked, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:16:53.61,1:16:58.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,a bunch of applications that that that the world, you know, has seen over the last few years, not just us, of course.
Dialogue: 0,1:16:58.57,1:17:01.77,Sub,,0,0,0,,But, I think over the next few years, like, there's going to be so many more cool things.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:01.82,1:17:03.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,And so, I I I mentioned some stuff.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:03.12,1:17:04.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, I'm very bullish on training.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:04.63,1:17:06.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm very bullish on fast jobs.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:06.55,1:17:12.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,I'm very bullish on, you know, certain things around like agents and and low latency and and things like that.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:12.13,1:17:16.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,But but like, in the end, we consider ourselves like an infrastructure shop, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:17:16.45,1:17:19.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, we we're not trying to build, you know, very narrow things.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:19.52,1:17:23.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, we try to build like very general-purpose building blocks and put them in front of customers.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:23.30,1:17:30.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then, those customers will combine those building blocks and build some incredible applications that we could have never anticipated.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:30.96,1:17:32.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,I want to go through some of your tweets.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:32.35,1:17:33.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,This will be the last part of it.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:33.92,1:17:34.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:34.36,1:17:37.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,and we can just like maybe you want to double-click on anything.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:37.15,1:17:40.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,A lot of long-running persistent sandbox stuff right now.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:40.11,1:17:41.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh today feels lift and shift.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:41.78,1:17:45.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,I suspect sandboxes will evolve from dev boxes in the cloud to something more ephemeral.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:45.92,1:17:50.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,Uh one environment for every command and snapshotting in between, so you can pause, resume, clone.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:50.88,1:17:52.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, I mean, we talked about this a lot.
Dialogue: 0,1:17:52.48,1:17:53.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, we talked a bit about it, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:17:53.80,1:18:01.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, I I I'm fully I I think it's I think the median sandbox in Modal today is like 8 seconds long or something like that.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:01.39,1:18:03.47,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, which is kind of incredible when you think about it.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:03.52,1:18:05.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and over time, I think it might get even shorter.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:05.92,1:18:07.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,We want to support long-running sandboxes, too.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:07.74,1:18:12.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, I think there are some like very valid use cases around long-running sandboxes.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:12.12,1:18:16.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,But like, my vision is like, you know, the world needs also we need to figure out this.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:16.12,1:18:21.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like, how can how can we just massively parallelize things and reducing the overhead of starting and stopping sandboxes.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:21.96,1:18:29.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,I don't know if I ran an IPO-ready startup engaged in capex arms race with a competitor, it would be nice to coordinate and pace the arms race a bit.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:29.24,1:18:31.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,No comment.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:31.76,1:18:35.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,The biggest downside of GPU scarcity is not that it's hard to get GPUs,
Dialogue: 0,1:18:35.50,1:18:41.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,but that almost all capacity is sold as 3 years as a 3-year rectangle starting 3 to 6 months in the future.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:41.39,1:18:43.04,Sub,,0,0,0,,That's not what demand looks like for most users.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:43.09,1:18:44.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think we talked about that a little bit as well.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:44.72,1:18:47.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, and you know, maybe just uh go a little bit deeper now.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:47.41,1:18:52.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like one thing I think Modal has always been very, very good at is fully on-demand capacity, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:18:52.36,1:18:56.72,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like if a customer comes to us and needs 100 GPUs, like we can typically give them that like immediately.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:56.77,1:18:58.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,You don't even have to talk to sales person.
Dialogue: 0,1:18:58.41,1:19:03.53,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a lot of workloads in in in real life, like they don't look like these like flat, you know, rectangular workloads.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:03.58,1:19:06.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like they scale up and down throughout the 24-hour cycle.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:06.17,1:19:07.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,They maybe grow over time.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:07.31,1:19:09.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,They maybe have certain burst periods.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:09.84,1:19:13.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,And to me, that is the challenge with a lot of customers today.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:13.05,1:19:16.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like the And And I do think it's a legit problem for for the space.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:16.56,1:19:20.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's like the only the prime way to get capacity is to to buy these rectangular blocks.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:21.00,1:19:21.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's not great.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:22.03,1:19:23.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,I think the world needs to evolve.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:23.35,1:19:29.73,Sub,,0,0,0,,And And that's fundamentally, I mean, I'm I'm I'm very biased, but like that's why I think Modal can play a very large role in in that sort of,
Dialogue: 0,1:19:29.78,1:19:35.17,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, whatever you want to call it, sort of duration transformation of offering more flexible capacity.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:35.22,1:19:41.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,And we can do that because because we can aggregate many, many thousands of customers and the demand gets very smooth in aggregate.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:41.07,1:19:44.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, that's a lot of the value prop of Modal is exactly that.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:44.08,1:19:45.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,Would you say that's a differentiator?
Dialogue: 0,1:19:45.44,1:19:46.35,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like I think so.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:46.40,1:19:46.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,Oh, really?
Dialogue: 0,1:19:46.93,1:19:47.23,Sub,,0,0,0,,Okay.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:47.20,1:19:48.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah, massive differentiator.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:48.55,1:19:52.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,I actually, in fact, like I think Modal's like, if you want to be like very sort of reductive,
Dialogue: 0,1:19:52.84,1:19:55.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,like you can break down Modal's value prop into two things.
Dialogue: 0,1:19:55.64,1:20:00.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,One is a developer experience, the ability like iterate and like build things and not have to think about infrastructure.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:00.76,1:20:02.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,The other thing is the capacity stuff.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:02.44,1:20:02.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,Yeah.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:02.68,1:20:07.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,The more and more I think about Modal and like where we're going in in the long run is like we're building like
Dialogue: 0,1:20:07.08,1:20:14.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,we're aggregating the world's compute capacity and we're very good at slicing it out like very granularly cuz we built all this work around,
Dialogue: 0,1:20:14.16,1:20:18.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,you know, scaling up and down extremely fast, you know, all the stuff around GPU snapshotting.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:18.12,1:20:21.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,We're working on some some ways to make it much, much faster.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:21.20,1:20:26.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,And that model works at very large scale, like really, really well cuz you can aggregate lots of customers.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:26.16,1:20:31.11,Sub,,0,0,0,,Every month I keep saying our same max revenue might apply to it, but it keeps going up 30% every month.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:31.16,1:20:32.03,Sub,,0,0,0,,It's kind of nuts.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:32.08,1:20:37.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like I don't under you know, like every month I'm like, "What well, you know, Sound boxes grew a lot, but like clearly they kind of plateau.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:38.03,1:20:39.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then they keep growing even more.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:39.95,1:20:41.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,So so I I I I don't know.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:41.61,1:20:45.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,And a lot of VCs like they're like how did you know Sound boxes was going to be big?
Dialogue: 0,1:20:46.03,1:20:48.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like and and the truth is like I didn't.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:48.51,1:20:52.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like like I I thought it was going to be bigger than it was in 2023 for sure,
Dialogue: 0,1:20:52.51,1:20:56.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,but like the fact that Sound boxes is a third of our revenue today, it's it's insane.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:57.04,1:20:59.12,Sub,,0,0,0,,And you know, we're obviously very happy about it.
Dialogue: 0,1:20:59.17,1:21:01.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,We we have like a very large team investing in Sound boxes.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:01.61,1:21:03.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,You know, I don't want to like trivialize it.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:03.56,1:21:11.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like we obviously I do want to give ourselves like a lot of credit for for seeing Sound boxes for what it is today a little bit back in even 2023.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:11.31,1:21:15.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I I I think you know, if like I probably would have never, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:21:15.65,1:21:20.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,taken a bet that revenue would be this large for Sound boxes and the market demand would be so much.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:20.84,1:21:25.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,There is an interesting structural reason why inference providers should exist separately from model builders.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:25.44,1:21:26.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,The best model keeps changing a lot.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:26.68,1:21:31.95,Sub,,0,0,0,,Inference providers keep can easily reallocate resources between models since overall demand is quite smooth.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:32.00,1:21:37.30,Sub,,0,0,0,,So I mean, if you think about all the Chinese labs and you know, in the future hopefully more US labs too,
Dialogue: 0,1:21:37.35,1:21:42.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,every time a new lab releases, you know, the latest and greatest open-source models.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:42.45,1:21:46.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,Like you see big shifts in demand, you know, for for these models.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:46.53,1:21:52.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,And and so I think a challenge with, you know, if all these labs independently like held capacity,
Dialogue: 0,1:21:52.76,1:21:55.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,they would see when they launch a new model, demand would go up a lot.
Dialogue: 0,1:21:55.84,1:21:59.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,And then another lab launches a better model and then the demand goes down, you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:21:59.48,1:22:01.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,but but of course like in the background they're working on the next version.
Dialogue: 0,1:22:01.91,1:22:02.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,So like I think you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:22:03.04,1:22:06.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,there's like a structural reason like I said like why you're going to start
Dialogue: 0,1:22:06.89,1:22:10.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,you're seeing demand kind of shifting to like the latest and greatest Chinese lab model.
Dialogue: 0,1:22:10.93,1:22:12.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,I mean, not like it's not like you know,
Dialogue: 0,1:22:13.04,1:22:17.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,this is like everything just moves overnight cuz cuz a lot of models and prompts and stuff
Dialogue: 0,1:22:17.60,1:22:21.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,like like a lot of things that get sort of there's like a certain stickiness to it.
Dialogue: 0,1:22:21.68,1:22:26.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,But I I do think you know, there's an argument that it's very easy for model to like, you know, right?
Dialogue: 0,1:22:26.57,1:22:33.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,Today we're running, you know, X number of thousand GPUs for one model, tomorrow we need a shift like, you know, 50% of that to another model.
Dialogue: 0,1:22:33.89,1:22:36.58,Sub,,0,0,0,,Sure, that's just like that's what the system was built to handle.
Dialogue: 0,1:22:36.63,1:22:38.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,No one even needs to tell us to do that.
Dialogue: 0,1:22:38.46,1:22:39.75,Sub,,0,0,0,,It just magically does that.
Dialogue: 0,1:22:39.80,1:22:44.19,Sub,,0,0,0,,So, you agree that basically the LLMs are commodities?
